1
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:04,880
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,080
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:10,380
My name is Catherine and today we are here with an advanced level lesson.

4
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:18,260
That means that we're going to listen to a pretty long and exciting dialogue and then we'll talk a little bit about what you've heard and some of these cool interesting phrases.

5
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,000
That's right.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,240
Today we have a lesson about planning a crime or planning a bank robbery.

7
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:23,480
Oh, my favorite.

8
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:28,920
There have been lots of movies about this topic, very fun movies.

9
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:32,440
From the beginning of movie making till now, it's a very popular topic.

10
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:33,440
That's right.

11
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:40,180
On this occasion, as Catherine said, it's a nice fun dialogue and we're going to have a lot of colloquial terms.

12
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,120
Maybe you won't really understand a couple of different phrases because they're very colloquial.

13
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:46,440
They're very American terms.

14
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:52,020
The accents are also, they're a little bit from Boston, kind of like New Jersey maybe or something.

15
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:52,690
Right.

16
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:57,070
So, keep an ear out for some of the strange or unusual phrases.

17
00:00:57,400 --> 00:00:59,680
We will be talking about them in a moment.

18
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,970
But don't worry, if you don't understand this dialogue the first time, you'll be hearing it again later.

19
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:13,440
All right.

20
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:15,490
Let's run through this one more time from the top.

21
00:01:16,290 --> 00:01:19,870
I will be positioned here, across from the bank on this park bench.

22
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:22,710
Now according to the intel we got from Jimmy.

23
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:24,330
Oh, who's Jimmy?

24
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:28,080
Geez, Ralph, pay attention, will ya?

25
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,150
Jimmy's our mole, you know?

26
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:30,550
The guy on the inside.

27
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,010
He's been snooping and passing on the info to us so we can pull this heist off.

28
00:01:35,570 --> 00:01:38,670
Yeah, Ralph, clean the mothballs out of your ears and listen up.

29
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,840
This here is important.

30
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:43,170
We don't want to end up back in the slammer, do ya?

31
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,190
Your role is pretty important here.

32
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:46,510
We're dependent on you, man.

33
00:01:46,910 --> 00:01:48,730
Okay, okay, I'm listening.

34
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:50,840
Mothballs.

35
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:52,130
Alrighty then.

36
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:53,250
Where was I?

37
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:58,690
Yeah, okay, so I'll be on the lookout, sitting here on the bench across from the bank.

38
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:03,120
Nobody moves until I give the go-ahead, all right?

39
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:04,150
And what's the go-ahead, Ralph?

40
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:10,350
You uh, you uh, oh yeah, you'll take off your hat and scratch your head.

41
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:11,110
Right.

42
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:15,210
When I take my hat off and scratch my head, you do what?

43
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:17,040
I get in the box.

44
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,490
Right, you get in the box.

45
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:24,970
I'll make sure it's all sealed and then posing as the delivery guy, I'll drop off a special package for the manager.

46
00:02:25,890 --> 00:02:31,170
Now according to Jimmy, the bank manager's leaving early on Tuesday, because it's his wedding anniversary.

47
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:41,100
He and the wife are having a romantic rendezvous in the country, so any packages delivered will be left unopened in his office until he gets back late on Wednesday.

48
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,460
Which gives us access to his office for at least 12 hours.

49
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:48,160
Okay Ralph, this is where you come in.

50
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:49,630
Where are you?

51
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:52,050
I'm standing right next to you, Sammy.

52
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:54,190
Oh sorry, Sam.

53
00:02:54,590 --> 00:02:56,330
I'm in the box, right here in that box.

54
00:02:56,750 --> 00:02:59,910
And what do you do once I deliver you to the manager's office?

55
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:04,790
I stay in the box until the bank is closed, and then I get out of the box.

56
00:03:05,270 --> 00:03:05,930
And then?

57
00:03:05,930 --> 00:03:07,790
What next, Ralph?

58
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:10,730
Oh for Pete's sake, this is never going to work.

59
00:03:11,270 --> 00:03:12,750
Hey, give me a chance here, fellas.

60
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:22,360
I crack the safe and then, then I take the money, and then I, um, I get back in the box.

61
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:24,480
Atta boy, Ralph.

62
00:03:24,490 --> 00:03:30,450
In the morning, I come back to the bank, say there's been a mix-up with the delivery I made, and take the special package back here.

63
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:32,610
Alright, let's get some sleep.

64
00:03:33,190 --> 00:03:34,770
It's a big day tomorrow, fellas.

65
00:03:38,190 --> 00:03:39,570
A perfect plan, Sammy!

66
00:03:39,970 --> 00:03:41,170
It went off without a hitch!

67
00:03:42,030 --> 00:03:45,990
Let's open this up and get Ralph out of here so we can start counting the money.

68
00:03:46,680 --> 00:03:50,120
Phew, I sure am glad to see you guys.

69
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:52,100
I was sure getting lonely with no one to talk.

70
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:52,980
That's nice.

71
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:53,960
Okay, how much?

72
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:55,400
Uh, really?

73
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:56,720
Really, really, really glad.

74
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:57,720
Money, Ralph, money!

75
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:58,720
Oh man, I knew I forgot something.

76
00:03:58,720 --> 00:03:59,720
Damn it.

77
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:00,720
Alright, we're back.

78
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:18,940
So, um, obviously this guy Ralph, I don't know why, always the one robber whose name is Ralph is not very clever.

79
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:25,340
No, it doesn't mean that Ralphs are generally not smart people, but yeah, I think you're right.

80
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:26,500
There's usually...

81
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:32,580
Yeah, like in bank robberies or something, like for example in the old movies, always Ralph was the one that was messing things up.

82
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:33,360
Oh, Ralph.

83
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:36,880
Well, let's find out what Ralph screwed up today.

84
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:42,690
First of all, we've got the guys Sammy, Ralph, and Frankie who are talking about the plan.

85
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:48,080
And Sammy mentions that he had the intel from Jimmy.

86
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:53,470
Okay, so when we talk about intel, we're talking about information, right?

87
00:04:53,920 --> 00:04:54,920
That's right.

88
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:59,370
So intel is a word that's short for intelligence or facts or information.

89
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:00,050
That's right.

90
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:11,900
So you can talk about intelligence as maybe you're very smart, you're very intelligent, but when we talk about intelligence, we talk about gathering information in order to help us accomplish an objective.

91
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:17,780
For example, in movies about the CIA, we often hear them talk about gathering intel.

92
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:19,780
That means collecting facts.

93
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,280
Right.

94
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,280
Very good.

95
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:26,720
So they had intel from Jimmy about the bank and the layout.

96
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:29,880
And well, they were like, who's Jimmy?

97
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:34,920
And Sammy says that, well, he's our mole, you know, the guy on the inside.

98
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:35,920
A mole.

99
00:05:35,920 --> 00:05:39,160
So mole is an animal.

100
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:47,350
But in cases of intelligence, or maybe in this case, a bank robbery, spying, a mole is a person who's basically stealing information.

101
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:57,390
So for example, if I work in the CIA, but I'm selling information from the CIA to the French government, I'm a mole.

102
00:05:58,160 --> 00:05:59,160
Right.

103
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,010
So you're basically like a spy.

104
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:01,880
Yes.

105
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:03,150
You're a person on the inside.

106
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:08,040
And that's what they mean when he says, he's our guy on the inside.

107
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:13,150
You're inside the enemy's territory or you're inside their organization.

108
00:06:13,770 --> 00:06:14,070
Exactly.

109
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:17,880
So for example, have you seen the show 24?

110
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:19,880
Yeah, with Jack Bauer.

111
00:06:20,090 --> 00:06:23,630
Well, they often have this problem of moles inside the organization.

112
00:06:24,250 --> 00:06:28,370
So maybe the terrorists have a guy on the inside.

113
00:06:28,730 --> 00:06:29,050
That's right.

114
00:06:29,210 --> 00:06:29,590
He's a mole.

115
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:32,910
So they have somebody that has penetrated their organization.

116
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:34,480
Exactly.

117
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:40,520
Okay, so they talk about the mole and how they are going to pull this heist off.

118
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:44,280
Okay, so the phrase here is to pull off a heist.

119
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:46,420
First of all, what's a heist?

120
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:50,400
Well, a heist is a robbery, is basically a robbery.

121
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:51,960
Yeah, it's another word for robbery.

122
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:53,440
It's kind of old though.

123
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,660
You don't really say it anymore, right?

124
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,180
No, you can say it's a heist movie or it's a movie about a heist.

125
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:01,440
It's almost like a genre.

126
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:02,520
Yeah.

127
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:09,760
So there's a basic storyline where they plan the robbery, they do the robbery, something goes wrong, what's going to happen?

128
00:07:09,760 --> 00:07:10,460
So that's a heist.

129
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:11,840
Very good.

130
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:14,980
So a heist is something that was planned out, like a robbery that's planned out.

131
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:17,920
It's not really if you come up to someone and say, give me your wallet.

132
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:18,800
That's not really a heist.

133
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:19,700
No, that's a stick up.

134
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:21,160
Right.

135
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:22,740
But a heist is something that you pull off.

136
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:25,100
So to pull off is to succeed.

137
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:26,120
To accomplish.

138
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:27,720
To accomplish.

139
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:28,720
So I pulled it off.

140
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:29,640
I was able to do it.

141
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:31,780
So you pulled off the heist.

142
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:32,820
Very good.

143
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:38,460
And well, they were talking about if they do it, they don't want to get caught because they don't want to go back to the slammer.

144
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:39,960
The slammer.

145
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:42,020
Yeah, well, I don't blame them.

146
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:43,660
I wouldn't want to go to the slammer either.

147
00:07:43,760 --> 00:07:45,360
So what is the slammer?

148
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:45,880
The slammer.

149
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:46,780
The big house.

150
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:47,660
Right.

151
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:50,040
Lock up, the slammer, the big house.

152
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:53,500
All these are colloquial synonyms for prison, for jail.

153
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:55,280
Jail.

154
00:07:55,280 --> 00:08:02,060
Normally we say prison or jail, like it's a maximum security prison, like prison break, the TV show.

155
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:03,680
Right.

156
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:05,680
But in colloquial English, we often say slammer.

157
00:08:05,680 --> 00:08:05,900
Okay.

158
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,620
The slammer or the big house.

159
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:08,680
Okay.

160
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:11,960
So they were talking that they don't want to go back to the slammer.

161
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:18,140
And now when they were actually planning out the crime, he says, well, you wait until I give you the go ahead.

162
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:18,920
Okay.

163
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:19,820
So this is the plan.

164
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:22,160
He's going to wait for a sign.

165
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:27,840
The go ahead means a sign that indicates that it's time to do something.

166
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:28,900
Okay.

167
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:32,320
So for example, so in this case, what is that sign?

168
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:38,240
The sign or the go ahead is he'll take off his hat and scratch his head.

169
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:38,760
Okay.

170
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:41,800
So that's a sign that means it's okay to proceed.

171
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:43,520
It's okay to continue.

172
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:48,060
So you can also say, okay, when we're ready to go, Marco, I'll give you the go ahead.

173
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:48,860
Okay.

174
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:50,400
Just wait for me to wave.

175
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:51,400
Okay.

176
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:52,400
Very good.

177
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:53,400
That's a signal.

178
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,400
Exactly.

179
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:55,400
All right.

180
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:56,400
Very good.

181
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:59,780
Now moving on, we have a French word here, but that's used very commonly in English.

182
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:03,500
And they said, okay, we have a rendezvous.

183
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:04,600
Okay.

184
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:10,780
So a rendezvous is a meetup or a time or place where people are connecting together.

185
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:20,100
So for example, in a heist, you could say we're going to rob the bank and then we will rendezvous at our favorite restaurant in two hours.

186
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:20,640
Okay.

187
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,420
So a rendezvous is to meet up, to get together.

188
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:24,720
Right.

189
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:26,860
But in this case, they're talking about a romantic rendezvous.

190
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:29,320
So this means a trip with a lover.

191
00:09:30,530 --> 00:09:31,690
So it's a little bit different, right?

192
00:09:31,910 --> 00:09:35,610
He and his wife are having a romantic rendezvous in the country.

193
00:09:36,050 --> 00:09:36,310
Okay.

194
00:09:36,390 --> 00:09:38,650
That means that they're going to have a romantic meeting.

195
00:09:39,090 --> 00:09:39,210
Okay.

196
00:09:39,870 --> 00:09:40,290
Very good.

197
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:44,450
And now this next phrase is very, very interesting.

198
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:47,670
It's very common to hear it as well.

199
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,960
I think like moms say it a lot.

200
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:51,110
Oh, for Pete's sake.

201
00:09:51,690 --> 00:09:52,950
Oh, for Pete's sake.

202
00:09:53,150 --> 00:09:54,570
Well, I don't know who Pete is, honestly.

203
00:09:55,720 --> 00:10:01,120
But this means my gosh or come on or I'm so tired of this.

204
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:03,650
So you're a little bit exasperated.

205
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:05,030
Maybe you're a little bit irritated.

206
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:07,590
Oh, for Pete's sake, Marco.

207
00:10:08,160 --> 00:10:08,810
Stop giving me examples.

208
00:10:09,290 --> 00:10:10,330
I'm just, I'm done hearing them.

209
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:12,000
Yeah.

210
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,430
So there are a lot of other ways to say this, right?

211
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:14,680
Yeah.

212
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:15,710
So you could say for goodness sake.

213
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,410
For goodness sake or oh, for heaven's sake.

214
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:19,940
For heaven's sake.

215
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:22,440
And finally, for God's sake.

216
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:23,440
Right.

217
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:27,180
But this one you have to be careful because some people who are religious might be offended if you use it.

218
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:33,000
So if that's the case, then you would just rather go with Pete's sake, heaven's sake or goodness sake.

219
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:33,460
Okay.

220
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:34,640
For goodness sake.

221
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:38,200
It's a very common phrase and as you say, it's for when you're a little bit irritated.

222
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:39,880
It's like oh, for goodness sake.

223
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:40,080
Stop complaining.

224
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:41,200
Exactly.

225
00:10:41,880 --> 00:10:42,880
Right?

226
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:43,880
Okay.

227
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:44,720
And now for our last word.

228
00:10:45,120 --> 00:10:47,000
You may have seen the word fellas.

229
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:47,800
Fellas.

230
00:10:49,370 --> 00:10:53,030
So this comes from the word fellow, person, man.

231
00:10:53,670 --> 00:11:06,230
But oftentimes in these kind of movies or stories about heists or crimes, the men talk to each other using the word fella or fellas.

232
00:11:06,610 --> 00:11:07,870
So come on, fellas.

233
00:11:08,130 --> 00:11:08,750
Let's go.

234
00:11:09,190 --> 00:11:10,210
That means come on, guys.

235
00:11:10,690 --> 00:11:11,190
Come on, everyone.

236
00:11:11,590 --> 00:11:11,810
Okay.

237
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:13,680
Yeah.

238
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:17,400
So this is always about men, not like a group of women would use this.

239
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:19,220
And it's something that's a little bit older.

240
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:22,680
We don't really say this like oh, me and my fellas, we're going to go to the store.

241
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:26,200
You would say this more in old time English.

242
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:27,200
Yeah.

243
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:32,480
You can see it maybe in some movies when somebody comes up and greets other people, other guys, he would say hey, fellas.

244
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:33,320
Exactly.

245
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:34,500
Or there's a mafia movie.

246
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:37,200
It's about the American mafia called Goodfellas.

247
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:38,360
Very good.

248
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:39,180
It's a very famous one.

249
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:44,160
So it kind of gives you that feeling of an older English like the 1940s to the 1970s.

250
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:45,160
Okay.

251
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:46,160
Very good.

252
